boot: harb init -- machine qemu_worker (hosted / x86_64 / qemu_pc) -- pid 1
boot: console /dev/ttyS0
boot: mount proc at /proc -- done
boot: mount sysfs at /sys -- done
boot: mount devtmpfs at /dev -- done
boot: mount cgroup2 at /sys/fs/cgroup -- done
boot: budget -- sandbox 32 MiB and 50% of a core; the kernel holds the ceiling, not the world
boot: confine -- asker has no sight of the other worlds and no way to start one, sandbox has no sight of the other worlds and no way to start one and no socket of any kind; what a world did not declare, the kernel does not give it
boot: floor -- the machine is not a world's to change: none may mount or unmount, set the clock, load a module, rename the host, make or enter a namespace, trace another process, take a priority over the other worlds, or reboot the box
boot: crypto -- the kernel answers a request for its crypto socket (AF_ALG) with EAFNOSUPPORT: it has none for a world to open, and the floor refuses one to every world besides
boot: state -- asker owns /run/asker as placer (2001:2001); each made, then read back as that identity's, mode 0700
boot: work -- sandbox runs once for each job asker places in /run/asker/jobs, as runner (2002:2002), for at most 3 s: it may write only a folder of its own, read only its program, make no socket of any kind, and what it prints goes to a file in that folder
boot: start asker -- pid N -- /harb job /run/asker/jobs one=probe two=probe three=spin four=eat -- as placer (2001:2001)
job: one -- placed: probe
work: sandbox -- job one taken from asker: 1 file in, a folder of its own
work: sandbox -- job one ended: exited 0; 4 files handed back to asker
job: one -- exited 0; 4 files handed back
job: one -- left: (empty)
job: one -- report: its folder holds, when it starts: stdout task
job: one -- report: a unix socket -- refused (PERM)
job: one -- report: an inet socket -- refused (PERM)
job: one -- report: a netlink socket -- refused (PERM)
job: one -- report: a socket pair -- refused (PERM)
job: one -- report: read /etc/machine -- refused (ACCES)
job: one -- report: list / -- refused (ACCES)
job: one -- report: make /run/escape -- refused (ACCES)
job: one -- report: start a process -- refused (PERM)
job: one -- report: read its task -- allowed
job: one -- report: make a file in its folder -- allowed
job: one -- report: make a folder in its folder -- refused (ACCES)
job: one -- report: every answer was the one a worker must get
job: one -- stdout: untrusted: this line is the worker's own, and it is written to a file in its folder, not to the console
